Saved: `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Frank-Lloyd-Wright/2026-08-26_135348/subject.md`

**Verdict:** trust `strained`, value `high`, delight `clear_hit`, recommendation **revise** — **5 blockers, 7 concerns**.

**Strongest hit (SUBJ-H1)** — the Welsh prophecy is attributed rather than asserted: *"At least, that is the story her son told,"* followed by the note that a book about self-invention is exactly where an origin myth would live. A piece arguing Wright composed a better version of his life is obligated to say when it's quoting that composition, and this one does — then lets the attribution strengthen the thesis instead of weakening it.

**Highest-priority finding (SUBJ-R1)** — the article reports an on-camera admission Wright never made. Lines 187 and 330 state *"He did not believe it… He told Mike Wallace he saw it."* What Wright actually said was *"Well, it's a pretty brittle shell"* — and in the same interview material, *"Any man who really has faith in himself will be dubbed arrogant by his fellows. I think that's what happened to me,"* and *"It isn't their job to build. It's mine."* The evidence packet independently flags the same overreach at E-01. A man can hold both that he's the greatest architect alive and that his armor is thin; the draft collapses them into a confession of fraudulence and sources it to the interview that contradicts it. The repair is small — cut the misreport, mark the inference as the article's reading, admit Wright's counter-framing — and it makes the argument better, because the tension it's currently flattening is the more interesting one.

The other four blockers: *"Wright never named another influence on his work"* is contradicted and self-contradicting (the draft credits Froebel ten lines earlier; Wright named the Japanese print in *An Autobiography*); the Miriam Noel grief timeline is tightened past the record twice; a nine-year-old is assigned a life-organizing decision built on the very myth the draft flagged; and the three men murdered at Taliesin were his draftsman, foreman, and gardener, not "three apprentices."

Two process notes worth surfacing beyond the review itself:

- **The SHA does not match, and it's benign.** The snapshot now hashes to `362a406e…`, not the declared `cae935d9…`. I verified independently before reviewing: the live draft still matches the declared SHA, and normalizing quote characters and emphasis markers reduces the snapshot-vs-live diff to zero substantive differences — the drift is Prettier plus the `label-paths` `path:` rewrite. Line numbering is unchanged, so citations are valid against both files. The evidence packet reached the same conclusion independently and documents it in its research limitations.
- **The evidence packet is unusually good** — its claim inventory (CLM-01–22) and dispute list did most of the factual work for me, which is why four of five blockers need no new research. Worth noting that its own largest flagged gap — that not one of Wright's load-bearing quotations was confirmed against a primary text — is real and constrains this review too.
